{"answer": "The critic's case rests on an equivocation between 'determinism' and 'confluence' and overreads a contingent caveat as a semantic defect.\n\n(1) The target's core semantics—the two-dimensional (status, rank) label computed as a least fixed point of a lexicographic product—is fully deterministic. A least fixed point of a monotone operator on a complete lattice is unique; there is exactly one such fixed point. Determinism of the fixed point is therefore preserved by construction, not merely 'conditionally recoverable.'\n\n(2) The phrase 'confluence can fail' does not refer to the fixed point itself but to the order in which a particular iterative procedure applies updates when ranks are not monotone. That is an algorithmic reachability concern, not a semantic indeterminacy: different update schedules may require canonical tie-breaking to converge to the same unique fixed point, but the fixed point they converge to is not in question.\n\n(3) The critic claims the determinism guarantee is 'contingent rather than preserved' because the canonical tie-breaking mechanism is 'not fully specified.' But the target states that tie-breaking 'must be canonical'; it does not leave tie-breaking optional at the semantic level. The existence of a constraint ('must be canonical or confluence can fail') is not the same as an admission of nondeterminism. It is a proviso naming a condition under which an implementation invariant holds. The semantic determinism of the least fixed point does not depend on the critic's demand that a specific tie-breaking rule be spelled out; it depends on the uniqueness of the least fixed point, which the lexicographic-product construction provides.\n\n(4) The critic has not established that 'preserved' requires preserving unrestricted algorithmic confluence under any evaluation order. The original problem requires determinism of the fixed point, not universal confluence of every iterative strategy. The target preserves the former; the latter is an implementation concern that the target explicitly flags and constrains.\n\nAccordingly, the critic has not shown that the semantics is nondeterministic, only that an implementation must respect a stated canonicality condition to ensure confluence—an operational caveat, not a concession of semantic indeterminacy."}